++From 1c142ec3f2541d1a0472e0ea96ba10dc180bb4ea Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
++From: DeltaVector1 <DeltaVector1@users.noreply.github.com>
++Date: Fri, 7 Aug 2026 06:57:15 +0000
++Subject: [PATCH 24/24] Tunnel watchdog: rate-limit rebuilds and probe ports in
++ parallel
++
++The /v1/models probe shares each tunnel with rollout traffic, so a BUSY tunnel
++misses probes while working, and it misses them on every link at once. Measured
++at 251 rollouts in flight: healthy counts tracked the batch phase (23/24 at the
++tail, 1/24 mid-batch) while both engines held 100% GPU and ~500 tok/s. The
++2-miss threshold with no cap then killed up to 17 live tunnels in one pass and
++destroyed every rollout on them: 68 kills and 139 rollout failures in an hour.
++
++Threshold 2 -> 6 misses, at most 2 kills per pass, 120s between passes. A
++deferred port keeps its miss count so it stays a candidate. This is the
++conclusion watch_rollout_links.sh already reached; do not tighten it under
++large batches.
++
++The probe loop also ran serially at 20s timeout, so one cycle took 2 to 7
++minutes rather than the nominal 45s interval, which made the miss threshold
++mean half an hour. It now probes all 24 ports at once and reads results through
++a here-string, not a pipe, so the misses array survives.
++
++Measured after: rollout failures fell from 194 to 64 per 5 minutes, cycle time
++from 2-7 minutes to about 65 seconds, and no live tunnel has been killed.
++
++Tests: 30, up from 26. The new cases cover the miss threshold, the per-pass
++cap, the cooldown, and that a deferred port stays a candidate.
++
++Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
++Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_017wiESxhziDrnsLnG9aizTd
